    "reasoning": "All 16 answers agree with their own captured worked solutions. q-121275: the product is negative when x>-2 and y>2, or x<-2 and y<2; option 3 shades these regions and excludes the boundary lines, matching MA's annotated solution image. q-83725: the coefficient of x+1 equals f'(-1)=3. q-83728 and q-83729: at the indicated centers, the graphs have positive value, positive slope, and negative concavity, requiring positive constant and linear coefficients and a negative quadratic coefficient. q-152877 and q-152230: integrating 2^x gives 2^x/ln(2), whose lower-limit contribution vanishes, yielding 24/ln(2) and 2/ln(2). q-20891: substituting u=sqrt(x) gives 2 times the integral of exp(-sqrt(2)u), yielding sqrt(2). q-50371: componentwise differentiation and the quotient rule give e^t/(e^t+1)^2 and 5t^4. q-34724: the product rule followed by sin(pi)=0 and cos(pi)=-1 gives components (-e^pi,e^pi,-e^pi). q-50467: differentiating twice gives 2sec^2(t)tan(t) and sec(t)(tan^2(t)+sec^2(t)). q-75034: the second derivatives are -3/(t+1)^2 and -1/(4t^(3/2)), for t>0; placing the minus sign inside the first numerator is equivalent to MA's display. q-135997: the first three binomial coefficients give 1-15c+375c^2/4, with the ellipsis representing the remaining terms. q-134165 and q-134173: using MA's explicitly stated population covariance convention, the sums of deviation products are 32 and 28, respectively; dividing by four gives 8 and 7. q-115060: isolating the root gives sqrt(x+2)>2, equivalent to x>2, which satisfies the domain. q-50202: integrating each component gives (e^t-4t,-cos(t)) plus an arbitrary constant vector. Confirmation is supported by the supplied worked solutions; no grading evidence was supplied.",
